WEBVTT

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.660
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place. I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:21.320
 In this fifth season, we're speaking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists about migrations of all kinds.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:31.760
 We'll hear about food and the experience of leaving home and in finding new ones, of decolonizing food traditions and tracing recipes through the movements of diaspora.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.740
 Delicious Revolution is made by Devon Samson and Chelsea Wills.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:40.680
 You can subscribe to the show on Apple Podcasts or wherever you get your podcasts.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:48.640
 And you can find all of our episodes along with pictures and more on the website, deliciousrevolutionshow.com.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:01:09.280
 Karla Diaz is an artist, writer, and activist. Born in Los Angeles and raised in both Mexico and LA, her work uses performance and writing to question institutional power, explore social practices and cultural relationships, create collaborations, and provoke dialogue among diverse communities.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:22.220
 She received her MFA from California Institute of the Arts and exhibited her work in local, national, and international venues, including MOCA, LACMA, The Whitney, ICA, Boston, and Serpentine Gallery in London.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:25.260
 She's co-director and founding member of Slanguage Studio.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:32.020
 Her ongoing project, Prison Gourmet, is a multimedia project informed by prisoners' recipes made from commissary food items.

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:35.880
 She recently received an Art Matters Award to continue its development.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:42.880
 In this episode, Karla talks with Chelsea about cooking in prison and the migration of recipes and food traditions in and out of incarceration.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:52.170
 Could you start with how, how did this project start for you?

00:01:52.940 --> 00:02:07.260
 So, it started in 2009, just thinking about it, you know, just in my studio, just, you know, in my own creative practice.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:08.500
 That's when I started for me.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:14.420
 But when I actually did the performance was in 2010 at LACMA.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.880
 It was like a two and a half hour performance, hour performance.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:33.840
 And they, the collective Fallen Fruit, which friends of ours, they had asked me to do a performance-based project as part of their, you know, taking over the museum and all this stuff.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:52.320
 So, to choose, it was really hard that, to make that decision because sometimes I feel like, you know, as artists, we, we want to touch upon things that are meaningful to us and, and subjects that, of course, you know, makes us and inspires our work and so forth and our processes.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:58.820
 But I had been pushing that for a long time because, you know, it was my brother.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.180
 It was a very personal and it was very particular.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:07.060
 And I didn't know if I wanted to kind of approach that, you know, and I had been like, yeah.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:08.860
 It's in the back and, you know, it's there.

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:13.360
 And I, I just felt like really frustrated as a human being and honest, I'm just being really honest, Chelsea.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:15.220
 So, I hope that's okay.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:15.840
 That's great.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:30.220
 Um, and I grew up, so I have, you know, two siblings and single mom and my, at that time, my mom also became really ill with, um, Parkinson's, was diagnosed with Parkinson's.

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:38.520
 So, I was having a really hard time because I became her caretaker and I had these younger siblings that I also had to be, you know, a mother to.

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:46.450
 So, I was like, you know, it was a frustrating time to be able to figure out, well, what do you do?

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:54.170
 So, I, I am the type of person that whatever scares me or, you know, and, and it's challenging.

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:59.510
 Of course, it's challenging, but I have to confront it because I feel like I always learn and grow from that.

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:00.370
 Do you know what I mean?

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:04.600
 Like, I think some people may have a different strategy, whatever.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:05.100
 I don't know.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:09.640
 But for me, it's like, it was terrifying, but at the same time, I was like, I have to confront it.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:11.220
 So, finally, anyway, long story.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:19.560
 Um, my brother went to, he was, he had been out in and out of prison, just getting in trouble and me trying to figure out all this stuff, trying to figure out the stuff with my mom.

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:28.700
 As I was her caretaker, um, full time and, you know, working, working, Chelsea, doing art projects.

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:48.100
 So, um, anyway, I realized that now, since he was going to be there for a longer period of time, you know, versus this kind of migration that you're talking about coming in and out of prison, um, that I had to buy food for him and sort of help him out and figure out what he could do.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:59.040
 He, he, he started to tell me about these canteen things, you know, that it's where you, the family, whoever can put money so that they can access those packages of food.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:05.720
 And I was going through these packages that they have from this company that, that they're the ones that bring the food, you know, to the prisoners and they have these packages.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:11.580
 And so I was like, you know, they're like $40 or 50, it's just different prices depending on what they have.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:15.400
 And so the packages, I was trying to find vegetables.

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:17.960
 Now I'm laughing about it.

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:23.040
 I wasn't laughing then, Chelsea, but, um, they didn't have vegetables.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:32.740
 They didn't have, it was like a package of like 10 cup of noodles, two like packages of like chocolate and candy.

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:34.660
 And then like hot Cheetos.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.390
 And I don't know, it was like that kind of package.

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:39.210
 And, um,

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:40.510
 You were like, where's the food?

00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:42.070
 Oh my God.

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:42.890
 That's what I said.

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:48.210
 I was like, oh my God, this knowing my brother, I was like, he's just gonna have a hard time going to the restaurant.

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:50.310
 This guy, you know, with like all this stuff.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:54.920
 So, um, anyway, so that's what happened.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:56.120
 And I said, you know what?

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:58.040
 And so he sent me a recipe.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:05.060
 So I bought him some stuff and he sent me a recipe from those commissary food items that it's not the cafeteria, but they get to purchase.

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:11.450
 Like I said, you know, those with the money that the families or whoever, you know, buys them for them.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:15.410
 Um, they get to select that and, and, and, and make those.

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:21.830
 And so they usually make those in their cell or in their own time, you know, not, it's not made and prepared in the cafeteria.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:24.860
 And so I thought, what the hell?

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:27.540
 It was so interesting to me, that recipe he sent me.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.520
 So that's how I started.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:34.500
 And I recreated that recipe, um, at LACMA in 2010.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:46.540
 And I'm telling you the, the feedback was just amazing to me because at that time, I mean, there were so many people, you know, museums, especially LACMA.

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:51.200
 Uh, they had buses of kids and people like coming, right?

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:59.580
 And I had samples of this and I think I also made a drink, one of the, um, the main drinks that a lot of, it's very more common.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:06.450
 I mean, now I know that it's more common, but it's like made with that, uh, sugary, you know, packages that they have.

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:10.530
 And so there were kids though, that, you know, they were like, what's that?

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:12.350
 And I said, Oh, you never try this.

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:13.510
 And I would give them a little bit.

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:14.850
 And I said, you're going to like it.

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:16.290
 Cause you know, it has a lot of sugar.

00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:19.850
 So the kids were coming back for seconds and thirds.

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:21.870
 And it was just crazy.

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:28.980
 Like we didn't have enough food, you know, and there were some of those language people, the artists that were helping me and Mario and my husband, you know, that were there.

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:37.200
 Um, in the meantime, I was trying to talk about not just, so the actual recreation of the recipe.

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:54.280
 My aim was to try to have people sample this and eat that, but also talk to them about the process and sort of like the limitations that they have with, you know, the, the cooking, the cooking tools that they have available to them.

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:57.210
 And how they're using those to make the food.

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:07.400
 So I would go through the process of, you know, just like I'm talking to you about telling them about the recipe and what it includes and the ingredients and then mixing it in.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:09.310
 And then what was the recipe?

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:13.660
 The, the recipe, what, um, the orange chicken recipe.

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:14.920
 Yeah.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:18.900
 So I would tell them and, um, what's in the orange chicken recipe.

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:24.230
 So the orange chicken has strawberry jelly, the chunky one.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:28.430
 And I, I, you know, I'm going to probably, that's why it's good that I have the recipes in front of me.

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:32.530
 But if I remember it has that, it has, uh, Kool-Aid has water.

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:39.250
 Um, and then it has, um, I'm sorry, I'm thinking of in Spanish it's, um, chicharron.

00:08:39.410 --> 00:08:40.110
 Do you know this word?

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:41.770
 Um, yeah, it's pork rinds.

00:08:41.790 --> 00:08:41.970
 Yes.

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:42.930
 Pork rinds.

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:44.450
 The pork rinds.

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:49.060
 Um, it has the noodles right as a base.

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:53.200
 Um, and essentially the, the sauces.

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:58.200
 So I would make, recreate the sauce, like the, the chunky sauce, the orange sauce for them.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:08:59.720
 And that's what they put in it to make it.

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:02.620
 But aesthetically, I thought it was amazing because.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:03.820
 But not to be rude.

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:06.160
 That sounds like the worst thing.

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:07.640
 It sounds so inedible.

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:12.030
 Yeah.

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:13.150
 Is it?

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:14.070
 What's it taste like?

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:20.170
 Well, I mean, it's with the sweet sauce and the, you know, the, the quick, the, the, the packages.

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:27.080
 It's, I mean, it's palatable, like in, you know, sweet, but, but it's still, I wouldn't need a lot of it.

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:29.200
 So that's why I was doing the little sample things, you know?

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:36.240
 And then I, I, um, I added, cause I wanted it to be gourmet kind of, you know?

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:38.950
 And so I added a little piece of pickle on the top.

00:09:38.950 --> 00:09:43.760
 So like, if you wanted to scoop it with the noodles, but, uh, what was interesting is that

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:49.580
 aesthetically, you know, in the recipe, the original recipe, like I could have, he, like

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:52.690
 I could have gotten just the strawberry jam, right.

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:58.700
 But he wanted it to be chunky so that it looked like the, like the actual orange chicken.

00:09:59.350 --> 00:10:06.070
 So does a recipe look like when you got that recipe, does it look like a recipe where like

00:10:06.070 --> 00:10:10.710
 there's the ingredients listed at the top and then, yeah, yeah, I would tell them.

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:15.190
 So that's what, so when he's, I said, you know, that's why I have to go back and, you

00:10:15.190 --> 00:10:21.140
 know, it's interesting too, because that's like part of that writing I have, you know,

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:23.580
 when I write to them and then, you know, they don't do it back.

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:29.100
 It's, it's, it's a very old system of communication with the prisoners, you know, honestly, like

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:29.820
 in that sense.

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:36.180
 Um, and so, but I would tell them, you know, what title of the recipe, like why you did it,

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:40.520
 the ingredients, you know, like, please, please just write it really separate so that I could

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:40.960
 have it.

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:47.120
 Because sometimes they, in their description and directions, you know, the recipes, you

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:51.080
 know, they don't get, they just say, oh, you steal this from like, like, you know, peppermint

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:56.760
 or if you don't have that, you can kind of steal that from the kitchen or there'd be like just

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:58.760
 their lingo and the talking about it.

00:10:58.820 --> 00:10:59.880
 They'd be like, oh, no worries.

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:05.050
 If you don't have, um, you know, um, a microwave, obviously, you know, depending on what level you're

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:11.350
 in to make the noodles and cook the noodles, they use trash bags, you know, they put hot

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:15.370
 water because they have hot water in their cells and access to that.

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:22.090
 And they, um, they put it in, they put the hot water in, in the bag and then they put the

00:11:22.090 --> 00:11:26.010
 noodles and then they close it, they sip it up.

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:32.640
 And then sometimes they even put a towel on it so that it like keeps it more, um, uh, warm

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:35.220
 so that it could cook faster, you know?

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:39.220
 So that kind of processes was really interesting to me how they would do that.

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:43.520
 And that was part of the recipes too, that they would describe what tools and.

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:44.390
 Yeah.

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:47.870
 And some of them were really funny, you know, I mean, they were like, some of them were

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:50.130
 like, I don't, I don't know what that means.

00:11:50.130 --> 00:11:50.950
 Or I don't know.

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:54.090
 We would go through them and be like, is that what it means?

00:11:54.110 --> 00:11:55.130
 That's really funny.

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:57.190
 And so you would have to try them out.

00:11:57.590 --> 00:12:02.810
 You, you get these recipes, you take them to your studio and then you get the things

00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:05.870
 together and then you try cooking whatever it was.

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:06.840
 Yeah.

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:08.280
 So that's what I do initially.

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:11.800
 So like when I get in, well, when I, you know, the orange chicken, I've done it now

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:16.190
 since that project, like I said, it's been since 2010 to now, right?

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:17.730
 Like it's been a couple of years.

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:21.380
 So I know, you know, the process a little bit more, right?

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:26.340
 And so I'll know what to kind of get, what to expect a little bit, you know?

00:12:26.780 --> 00:12:34.190
 But, um, but if it's a new recipe, you know, I have to go through it first.

00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:36.430
 I read it and make sure that I understand it.

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:41.410
 And I go through it and I kind of do it in my kitchen, in my house here, um, to kind of

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:44.480
 do the steps and see if this makes sense.

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:45.220
 You know what I mean?

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:53.260
 And then, um, and then I, if I want to select it for like the possibly doing it, that I

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:59.140
 think it's an interesting recipe, then I'll go ahead and try to make it, you know, um, and

00:12:59.140 --> 00:13:01.300
 have Mario is so bad.

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:03.620
 Cause he's like, I don't, don't give me any of that, you know?

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:08.700
 So I'm kind of usually the tester of what, what the final thing is.

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:10.680
 Well, that's my, that's my question.

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:12.140
 Like, how do you know if you got it right?

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:15.300
 You know, and it's about communication.

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:22.230
 So sometimes I've had to rewrite back to the, cause I'm, I have to be in communication with

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:22.830
 them, you know?

00:13:23.090 --> 00:13:27.560
 And so if I have to write back and be like, is this what you, sometimes I don't, I can't

00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:29.100
 even read the writing, you know?

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:31.330
 And, uh, is that how it is?

00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:36.870
 And of course I'm never going to know a hundred percent, you know, what is that the right taste,

00:13:36.990 --> 00:13:37.470
 you know?

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:44.540
 Um, but, but they're really, you know, and sometimes they don't even have measuring stuff.

00:13:44.540 --> 00:13:46.680
 Like, I don't know how much water to put.

00:13:46.820 --> 00:13:50.760
 I don't know how much, like they don't, if they don't do like ounces or, you know, anything

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:51.260
 like that.

00:13:51.260 --> 00:13:56.370
 Like I have to write back and be like, well, is that going to be too watery?

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:57.570
 Like, I don't know.

00:13:57.670 --> 00:14:01.190
 You said this much, but how, how much do you think I should put?

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:02.870
 You know what I mean?

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:09.630
 Um, because they're not thinking like cooks, they're not thinking like you need this of

00:14:09.630 --> 00:14:11.850
 this ounces, you know, and right.

00:14:11.950 --> 00:14:14.070
 They are not in that sense.

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:17.770
 They're just putting those elements and ingredients together, you know?

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:25.060
 Um, which was really interesting too, because there is a recipe that is very common that they

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:25.700
 put together.

00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:30.640
 Um, and they all share the, the prisoners, some of the prisoners, they all put elements of

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:33.990
 that meal together and they call it the spread.

00:14:34.130 --> 00:14:39.900
 And essentially it's just them, you know, picking a sheet, whatever they have, you know,

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:44.640
 a trash bag, putting it, laying it all out and say, okay, there's, let's spread it out.

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:46.400
 Let's spread all what we have.

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:46.680
 Right.

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:47.740
 Because it's about resources.

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:50.880
 A lot of time it's about resources in prison.

00:14:50.880 --> 00:15:01.090
 And so, um, you know, if I have left from whatever, you know, my, my package that I got three cup

00:15:01.090 --> 00:15:03.430
 of noodles and that's it, I got nothing.

00:15:03.490 --> 00:15:04.170
 I don't have meat.

00:15:04.230 --> 00:15:08.920
 I don't have, you know, nothing, you know, what can you offer so that we could add together

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:11.740
 and then mix it and we can make a meal together, you know?

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:19.690
 And I was thinking that as much as we want to know, and I understand the politics, all

00:15:19.690 --> 00:15:26.800
 the stuff of like how segregated, you know, prison is that there's like these odd holes,

00:15:26.940 --> 00:15:37.410
 these loopholes of collaboration, you know, and communal meals that they're not addressing,

00:15:37.570 --> 00:15:40.120
 but they're not, you know, they probably don't even know.

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:41.640
 It's just like, Hey, let's eat, you know?

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:47.700
 Well, I think like as people who aren't in prison, food is one of those things that brings

00:15:47.700 --> 00:15:48.940
 us together all the time.

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:49.420
 Right.

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:49.800
 Yeah.

00:15:50.460 --> 00:15:56.340
 In really like everyday mundane ways, like you having a cup of coffee with your husband or

00:15:56.340 --> 00:16:00.840
 something like that to like birthday parties or holidays or things like that.

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:06.980
 And it seems like one of the hardest things to me would be like being on someone's schedule.

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:08.210
 Yeah.

00:16:08.210 --> 00:16:14.340
 Like you, like you are in prison and then also having no choice about what those things

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:14.720
 are.

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:15.120
 Right.

00:16:15.320 --> 00:16:15.840
 So, yeah.

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:22.330
 So that's what's really interesting too, because, uh, I've been really blessed in that sense.

00:16:22.330 --> 00:16:29.330
 I say that honestly, because I've been able to be blessed to work with, um, different guides,

00:16:29.490 --> 00:16:35.820
 different prisoners, um, in different levels of the correctional, you know, institutional system

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:36.220
 or whatever.

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:43.940
 And so, uh, you know, I, the lower kind of level entry prisoners have access to microwaves,

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:45.380
 have access to those things, you know?

00:16:45.820 --> 00:16:51.700
 Um, but then again, I've had the experience of doing one of the recipes from a guy who was

00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:57.100
 in the SHU program, who is the maximum security prison in here in one of the institutions in

00:16:57.100 --> 00:17:01.720
 California and the SHU program security housing unit, they call it.

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:07.480
 So he was there 23 hours a day on lockdown and they only leave him.

00:17:07.600 --> 00:17:11.010
 I think it's like an hour to get out, to go and exercise.

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:18.390
 But where he exercises is essentially it's outdoors, but it's a cage, you know, it's, it's a cage

00:17:18.390 --> 00:17:20.260
 within another cage.

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:22.880
 He's by himself in that little cage.

00:17:23.390 --> 00:17:31.070
 Um, and so his experience, you know, in terms of making the food and eating all this stuff

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:35.990
 in his contact with some of the guys, he had, he had no contacts available.

00:17:35.990 --> 00:17:40.570
 I mean, you know, within the prisons because usually they get to see other prisoners, whatever,

00:17:40.570 --> 00:17:44.880
 but he couldn't, he didn't have for like three, four years.

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:52.370
 He was in the SHU, so he was behind, even if he would tell me when his family, his mom

00:17:52.370 --> 00:17:54.970
 would go visit him, he would be, you know, behind the glass.

00:17:54.970 --> 00:17:56.090
 So no contact.

00:17:56.850 --> 00:18:02.580
 And just as a side note, and I'm not, I'm not going to mention names, nothing, but I'm

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:04.540
 just generally through him.

00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:11.310
 It was a different experience of being able to relate to, to that, you know, to food, to

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:15.670
 even within that, because he, he didn't have, he didn't see anybody really.

00:18:15.670 --> 00:18:21.310
 I mean, it was just man, him, himself, because he was under that regulation and he got in

00:18:21.310 --> 00:18:21.670
 trouble.

00:18:21.890 --> 00:18:26.820
 He said, you know, like I said, I don't know, but he got in trouble because they found a

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:30.820
 drawing that someone had given him of a son.

00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:32.820
 I think it was like an Aztec son or something.

00:18:33.500 --> 00:18:40.560
 And that, uh, prison officials and the people said that that was an iconography that meant

00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:46.120
 that he was in part of the Mexican mafia and he was related and tied into that.

00:18:46.120 --> 00:18:52.870
 So I started to think about that at that time, which brought another question to me.

00:18:52.930 --> 00:18:54.920
 I had to be really careful.

00:18:54.920 --> 00:19:01.330
 I have to be really, really careful when I'm working with these guys in these institutions

00:19:01.330 --> 00:19:04.050
 because they could really get in trouble.

00:19:04.460 --> 00:19:13.330
 You know, anything like that, any misinterpretation of something could, uh, you know, hurt them, could

00:19:13.330 --> 00:19:19.570
 be confused, could be taken somewhere else, you know, and I, I really have to tread really

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:21.690
 carefully in that sense, you know?

00:19:22.240 --> 00:19:24.400
 Um, how do you, how do you do that?

00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:30.000
 I mean, because you're developing relationships with people as people, right?

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:33.940
 So you, do you talk about more things than just food?

00:19:34.160 --> 00:19:40.810
 I mean, you, you, I mean, you know, daily, because I started my experience, I think what

00:19:40.810 --> 00:19:47.340
 happened, I had an insider in a way, an insider way of approaching that, right?

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:52.870
 Because it was my brother, you know, because trust is such a big, big issue.

00:19:52.960 --> 00:20:00.370
 Like it's, it's like, they have to trust that you are not gonna hurt them because they, they

00:20:00.370 --> 00:20:02.450
 don't, they can, that's one of the major things.

00:20:02.470 --> 00:20:07.550
 There's always so skeptical, you know, and, um, and, and right enough, you know, I mean,

00:20:07.550 --> 00:20:17.130
 I, I'm not saying that they're not, but, um, but so, so I think it's about developing that

00:20:17.130 --> 00:20:23.550
 trust and, and that's one of the things if I, so then I kind of had to shift because I

00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:28.590
 knew, so some of the guys that my brother or friends or people knew, then he knew and

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:34.230
 they recommended and somebody else I admit, then he had told his cellmate or the, Hey,

00:20:34.230 --> 00:20:35.630
 maybe you want to include in this.

00:20:35.730 --> 00:20:39.850
 And so then they started organizing themselves and trying to send me recipes.

00:20:40.030 --> 00:20:40.630
 Do you know what I mean?

00:20:40.910 --> 00:20:42.370
 So they trust each other.

00:20:42.450 --> 00:20:45.350
 They know each other and therefore they will trust me.

00:20:45.470 --> 00:20:46.130
 You know what I mean?

00:20:46.270 --> 00:20:46.510
 Yeah.

00:20:46.970 --> 00:20:47.170
 Yeah.

00:20:47.270 --> 00:20:54.750
 Um, and so I think always with these, I think my background in terms of me growing up and

00:20:54.750 --> 00:21:00.400
 being able to be in the neighborhood and growing up in the streets and just having a lot of

00:21:00.400 --> 00:21:07.300
 that, uh, really is a skill that I think has helped me in that sense, you know, with being

00:21:07.300 --> 00:21:16.910
 able to work with, um, you know, these prisoners who I'm not, I think about like how developing

00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:20.030
 new relationship, let's say with these women that I don't know them, you know, I'm going

00:21:20.030 --> 00:21:23.400
 to New Orleans and that's a very different strategy.

00:21:23.400 --> 00:21:26.090
 You know, it has to be because they don't know me.

00:21:26.600 --> 00:21:27.680
 They don't trust me.

00:21:28.170 --> 00:21:29.880
 You know, why should they, right?

00:21:30.080 --> 00:21:31.440
 I mean, who are you?

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:33.120
 No, that's what I said.

00:21:33.220 --> 00:21:34.480
 And we have an issue of conversation.

00:21:34.780 --> 00:21:42.840
 So it's a hard thing to build, you know, and that's why this projects like this take time,

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:49.690
 you know, because you are sort of having an investment and taking the time to know each

00:21:49.690 --> 00:21:54.120
 other and to be able to say, you know, what are you trying to do?

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:54.940
 What do you want to do?

00:21:54.980 --> 00:21:55.760
 What is this for?

00:21:55.840 --> 00:21:56.620
 How do we do that?

00:21:56.680 --> 00:22:00.480
 You know, I think that's one of the major things to be able to trust each other.

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:04.280
 And at the same time, I always tell them, if you don't want me, you don't want me to

00:22:04.280 --> 00:22:09.140
 put any names in here, can be anonymous, you know, just tell me how I could, you know,

00:22:09.200 --> 00:22:14.140
 I want to make sure that you are also valued that this is yours, but how do I do that, that

00:22:14.140 --> 00:22:17.520
 you will be satisfied with, or would you be available?

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:21.580
 Would you be willing to contribute even though I may not have your information, you know?

00:22:22.720 --> 00:22:26.670
 And a lot of them have said, yeah, yeah, no, don't put any of no worries.

00:22:26.670 --> 00:22:34.410
 Well, so that's, that's a pretty interesting thing as an artist, like that conversation about

00:22:34.410 --> 00:22:36.530
 authorship comes up a lot, right?

00:22:36.650 --> 00:22:43.820
 In terms of like, I think, especially, you know, with the sort of work that you do, not

00:22:43.820 --> 00:22:48.590
 just, not just the work in prisons, but the work with language and other projects.

00:22:49.590 --> 00:22:58.120
 A lot of times, socially based artists are grappling with the issue of like, how we are

00:22:58.120 --> 00:23:02.750
 in conversation with our collaborators, and how authorship is shared, right?

00:23:04.070 --> 00:23:09.590
 And, you know, it's much easier for me to think of like, bad examples of that than good examples,

00:23:09.690 --> 00:23:13.390
 because good examples require so much more finesse.

00:23:13.990 --> 00:23:19.360
 But, I mean, in a certain way, you're doing this, like you are the spokesperson, inadvertently,

00:23:19.810 --> 00:23:21.900
 right, for these people's lives.

00:23:21.960 --> 00:23:22.780
 What is that like?

00:23:22.880 --> 00:23:24.060
 How do you negotiate that?

00:23:25.010 --> 00:23:32.220
 Yeah, and I try, it's really hard, because, you know, I don't want to, I don't want to

00:23:32.220 --> 00:23:36.140
 represent them wrong, you know, I don't, I don't want to represent anybody other than

00:23:36.140 --> 00:23:43.850
 like, I feel like us, you know, something like a, I think it's a valuable experience to

00:23:43.850 --> 00:23:47.750
 begin to tell each other our stories and our own stories.

00:23:47.750 --> 00:23:53.780
 And I believe that, you know, we should, we should share those, you know.

00:23:54.080 --> 00:24:01.910
 And so for me, my job, I have to always think about, because I am not a social worker, because

00:24:01.910 --> 00:24:04.990
 I'm not my, I'm not a missionary, you know what I mean?

00:24:04.990 --> 00:24:07.750
 Like, I'm not a priest trying to convert these prisoners.

00:24:07.750 --> 00:24:12.200
 And, you know, I'm not, that's not my, my goal, my intent.

00:24:13.390 --> 00:24:19.360
 So I have to always, when it gets tricky in that sense, like, how am I doing this?

00:24:19.400 --> 00:24:21.200
 How should I go about doing this?

00:24:21.200 --> 00:24:27.660
 Or, or when opportunities come into play, would you be willing to do this, whatever, blah,

00:24:27.700 --> 00:24:27.940
 blah, blah.

00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:32.180
 I have to reflect and say, I'm an artist.

00:24:32.420 --> 00:24:34.610
 And what am I trying to do?

00:24:34.690 --> 00:24:35.830
 What's the intent?

00:24:35.830 --> 00:24:38.650
 And I'm operating under that umbrella.

00:24:39.010 --> 00:24:45.550
 And it reinforces that because as an artist, I don't have to be correct.

00:24:45.790 --> 00:24:47.550
 I don't have to find a solution.

00:24:48.030 --> 00:24:55.280
 I don't have to even, you know, finish anything.

00:24:55.600 --> 00:25:03.090
 You know, my job in this project, the intent was to question, you know, and I think that's

00:25:03.090 --> 00:25:08.310
 such a key thing in terms of like a creative art, artistic practice in that sense.

00:25:08.310 --> 00:25:12.030
 Like I am seeing things visually.

00:25:12.030 --> 00:25:20.350
 I am seeing something that needs to be valued or pointed to or recognize or question.

00:25:20.430 --> 00:25:25.160
 And then you can come up with all the answers you want, or you can make your own interpretations.

00:25:25.560 --> 00:25:26.240
 Do you know what I mean?

00:25:26.900 --> 00:25:36.880
 And if there is something like these subcultural things, practices, or things that are being

00:25:36.880 --> 00:25:43.110
 happening, hidden underneath, underground, whatever, I really like that because I, I, I like to

00:25:43.110 --> 00:25:46.470
 point out to those, you know, and say, Hey, what's going on here?

00:25:46.470 --> 00:25:51.680
 Or, you know, Oh, that's interesting, you know, and I think we have to always come back to

00:25:51.680 --> 00:25:51.800
 that.

00:25:51.880 --> 00:25:54.950
 I'm not here to, again, solve the issue.

00:25:55.350 --> 00:26:01.110
 And, and I, I've had experiences where we had the performances and I'm telling you as much

00:26:01.110 --> 00:26:06.250
 as I've had a lot of positive feedback, I've also had a lot of negative feedback, you know,

00:26:06.350 --> 00:26:13.600
 and mainly has been like, you know, from people who are, his families have been hurt by people

00:26:13.600 --> 00:26:16.810
 who are in prison, you know, which I totally understand.

00:26:16.970 --> 00:26:20.970
 And, and they'll come in and be like, good thing that they're, um, that they're eating

00:26:20.970 --> 00:26:21.570
 that crap.

00:26:21.690 --> 00:26:21.930
 Good.

00:26:22.030 --> 00:26:24.170
 They should eat more and give them hell.

00:26:24.170 --> 00:26:27.680
 And, you know, and I've been thrown things that, you know, back.

00:26:28.140 --> 00:26:35.610
 And, um, so, and I tell them, you know, and I come back to it and I say, I'm not here to

00:26:35.610 --> 00:26:37.590
 judge them in terms of the crime, whether it's right.

00:26:37.650 --> 00:26:39.960
 I do not, I do not even touch that.

00:26:40.100 --> 00:26:45.100
 I do not try to even go on that side because that's not my intent.

00:26:45.180 --> 00:26:46.280
 I'm not a social worker.

00:26:46.280 --> 00:26:50.820
 I'm not a judgmental, uh, a person who's going to judge that, you know?

00:26:51.080 --> 00:26:56.500
 And so I think when I come back to that and sort of being able to recreate that, I feel

00:26:56.500 --> 00:27:04.900
 like I can think of my role more as a facilitator and an artist than being able to say, I am

00:27:04.900 --> 00:27:08.610
 here to, you know, make this seem the right way.

00:27:08.610 --> 00:27:10.910
 Like your chance, how do you know it tastes right?

00:27:10.970 --> 00:27:12.710
 You know, it's like, or how they want it to taste.

00:27:12.790 --> 00:27:13.910
 Well, maybe it's not.

00:27:13.950 --> 00:27:18.290
 It probably is like, you know, it's, it's, their recipe is much better, you know, how

00:27:18.290 --> 00:27:25.210
 it actually tastes, you know, but, um, but it's being able to sort of facilitate that,

00:27:25.410 --> 00:27:30.130
 you know, um, that I think it's, it's what I'm, I'm trying my intent.

00:27:30.230 --> 00:27:31.710
 Does that make sense, Chelsea?

00:27:31.890 --> 00:27:32.150
 I don't know.

00:27:32.230 --> 00:27:32.430
 Yeah.

00:27:32.430 --> 00:27:33.830
 I think that really makes sense.

00:27:33.950 --> 00:27:35.750
 I think, well, I love that.

00:27:35.830 --> 00:27:38.950
 I love that as a description of the role of an artist in society.

00:27:38.950 --> 00:27:44.850
 I might just have to borrow that and use it all the time, but I think the idea of, um,

00:27:45.620 --> 00:27:51.940
 it being a provocation rather than a statement, you know, is so, it's so important.

00:27:52.340 --> 00:27:53.980
 And I think you're right.

00:27:54.100 --> 00:27:57.220
 I mean, you, you have a very clear role in that.

00:27:57.220 --> 00:28:03.900
 And I think what an incredible thing to keep stating that over and over again, like you have

00:28:03.900 --> 00:28:12.020
 picked something that people have a lot of opinions about, including you, you know, like

00:28:12.020 --> 00:28:13.680
 you're not excused from that.

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:14.640
 Yeah.

00:28:15.060 --> 00:28:18.520
 I mean, you know, and it happens as, as artists, right.

00:28:18.620 --> 00:28:22.470
 As, as great, as creative people, we have our own judgments of things.

00:28:22.510 --> 00:28:23.270
 I mean, of course, right.

00:28:23.290 --> 00:28:28.930
 We have experiences, we have things, but, um, but I always feel like I'm just really, I tread

00:28:28.930 --> 00:28:37.290
 carefully that I'm not, you know, promoting something negative, something derogative, something,

00:28:37.850 --> 00:28:41.960
 you know, in that sense, because I'm, that's not my aim.

00:28:41.960 --> 00:28:45.740
 And if I would do that, I would totally go back and be like, okay, wait a minute, what's

00:28:45.740 --> 00:28:46.120
 going on?

00:28:46.510 --> 00:28:52.630
 Uh, there's, because I feel that doesn't even add anything as a, as a, as a, as an artist,

00:28:52.670 --> 00:28:54.010
 like, what am I trying to do?

00:28:54.010 --> 00:28:57.410
 You know, am I just drawing this stuff out?

00:28:57.630 --> 00:28:59.210
 And what, what am I saying?

00:28:59.210 --> 00:28:59.530
 Right.

00:28:59.930 --> 00:29:04.990
 So, so I, I always try to tread as carefully as I can.

00:29:05.070 --> 00:29:10.690
 I mean, I've had people like ask me, you know, do you want to be somebody from Canada?

00:29:10.850 --> 00:29:16.090
 You know, producer reached out to me and was like, do you want to do a prison gourmet show,

00:29:16.210 --> 00:29:23.670
 you know, and, and here, um, in Canada and see if we could do a segment on prison food.

00:29:23.990 --> 00:29:28.970
 And so I would have to do, think back, let's say an opportunity like that and say, okay,

00:29:29.070 --> 00:29:35.180
 this is going to be a massive television, you know, think, how is that, you know, my intent?

00:29:35.380 --> 00:29:36.260
 How is that coming?

00:29:36.400 --> 00:29:37.280
 How is this going to work?

00:29:37.420 --> 00:29:43.200
 Because I think the more I have an experience with this, there is a sense of that responsibility

00:29:43.200 --> 00:29:51.300
 of, you know, what these guys, that's their, their, their recipes, I'm not the cook, you

00:29:51.300 --> 00:29:52.260
 know, I'm not the food.

00:29:52.360 --> 00:29:55.280
 I'm just, I am kind of facilitating this, you know?

00:29:55.520 --> 00:29:57.440
 And so I have to go back.

00:29:57.460 --> 00:30:00.800
 And when something like that happens, I have to say, let me check.

00:30:01.560 --> 00:30:02.140
 Do you know what I mean?

00:30:02.180 --> 00:30:06.000
 Like I have to go back and say, this is on the proposal.

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:06.920
 This is what's happening.

00:30:06.920 --> 00:30:12.160
 And, and sometimes, you know, as artists, if we're not dealing with that, you could just

00:30:12.160 --> 00:30:13.500
 make that decision yourself, right?

00:30:13.520 --> 00:30:15.220
 You have a part, you're like, yeah, go ahead.

00:30:15.280 --> 00:30:15.800
 Let's go for it.

00:30:15.820 --> 00:30:16.080
 Woo!

00:30:16.180 --> 00:30:20.920
 You know, television, you know, and I'm making money and, you know, and I'm like, no, you

00:30:20.920 --> 00:30:23.580
 know, wait a minute, wait a minute, what's going on, you know?

00:30:24.140 --> 00:30:31.140
 So I have to always, again, be able to reflect.

00:30:31.280 --> 00:30:37.120
 It's, it's such a reflective, uh, sort of journey for me, you know, because I always have to

00:30:37.120 --> 00:30:39.930
 keep coming back to that intent.

00:30:40.320 --> 00:30:45.170
 Um, and I know there's a lot of hours, there's a lot of people, there's a lot of great organizations

00:30:45.170 --> 00:30:49.270
 that are working with, you know, prisoners that have been more throughout.

00:30:49.270 --> 00:30:55.530
 And now, you know, I have to say, you know, sadly, there's a lot more people in prison.

00:30:56.350 --> 00:31:01.690
 I know before, you know, a couple of, even a couple of years when I was a teenager, you

00:31:01.690 --> 00:31:06.230
 know, I was really embarrassed to really talk about that.

00:31:06.330 --> 00:31:10.250
 If I was like, you know, oh my God, like they're in prison, they have, or have a family member

00:31:10.250 --> 00:31:18.620
 that in prison, you know, because it's, it wasn't part of us almost like, oh, hey, now

00:31:18.620 --> 00:31:20.580
 they're being institutionalized for anything.

00:31:20.860 --> 00:31:25.540
 Young people, a lot of young people are being criminalized at a very young age, you know,

00:31:25.640 --> 00:31:32.770
 for things that I think my perspective, you know, don't deserve that kind of criminality

00:31:32.770 --> 00:31:33.890
 at that age.

00:31:34.010 --> 00:31:39.190
 So for instance, the high school here, the local high school here where we live, you know,

00:31:39.250 --> 00:31:45.390
 when the kids are late for a few minutes, the cops come and they give them a ticket, you

00:31:45.390 --> 00:31:45.570
 know?

00:31:47.520 --> 00:31:48.920
 That's pretty severe.

00:31:48.920 --> 00:31:52.940
 And they, and they, yeah, they take them, they tear the, you know, they detain them and

00:31:52.940 --> 00:31:54.370
 they give them a ticket.

00:31:54.570 --> 00:32:02.220
 And so my question comes into being like that never, I mean, actually my experience, I, you

00:32:02.220 --> 00:32:05.120
 know, I never got a ticket when I was late.

00:32:05.120 --> 00:32:08.400
 I mean, some of the, you're, you're a kid, you're in the buzz, you're, you know, you got

00:32:08.400 --> 00:32:08.840
 to get there.

00:32:08.860 --> 00:32:13.740
 And then it's like, you couldn't make it, you know, you overslept, you know, in thinking

00:32:13.740 --> 00:32:19.770
 about, well, how would that, what other alternative educational things, right?

00:32:19.770 --> 00:32:25.350
 That we could do maybe to help the students or things that we could do that, that will

00:32:25.350 --> 00:32:26.770
 help them get there, right?

00:32:27.010 --> 00:32:30.650
 Get there early, wake us up or talk to his parents, you know, the family.

00:32:30.990 --> 00:32:34.570
 But, but no, instead he gets a ticket.

00:32:35.830 --> 00:32:40.740
 So, well, it's not, it's not like you don't have a position on this.

00:32:40.840 --> 00:32:41.820
 That's not what you're saying.

00:32:42.020 --> 00:32:43.040
 Like, right, right.

00:32:43.160 --> 00:32:44.620
 Clearly you're a whole person.

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:47.410
 You have a whole creative practice.

00:32:47.550 --> 00:32:52.080
 Lots of your work is, yeah, I don't claim to know like every piece of work you've ever

00:32:52.080 --> 00:32:57.260
 made or something, but you, you, you, you know, you're political, right?

00:32:57.260 --> 00:33:03.400
 Whether, whether or not it's like always clearly taking a stand.

00:33:03.500 --> 00:33:07.780
 You've worked in your neighborhood for your whole life, you know, obviously you don't

00:33:07.780 --> 00:33:11.120
 want everybody to get arrested and be shipped off to prison all the time.

00:33:11.260 --> 00:33:13.000
 That's not what you're about.

00:33:13.680 --> 00:33:13.880
 No.

00:33:14.140 --> 00:33:20.000
 And, and I think, you know what, and I have decided like, um, I think certainly, yeah, I,

