 And so they, the curator, Josh Hoon said to me, uh, that he wanted me to have an installation

00:39:14.740 --> 00:39:21.150
 of, of, of the prison gourmet and some of the menus or recipes from the prisoners.

00:39:21.810 --> 00:39:26.120
 And he had told me about this movie and I'm going to blank out.

00:39:26.180 --> 00:39:27.540
 I'm trying to remember the name of the movie.

00:39:27.540 --> 00:39:38.130
 It was like a French movie that, um, a documentary that, uh, he said was made by a, um, uh, French

00:39:38.130 --> 00:39:46.100
 documentary that was made that they, they interviewed a lot of the survival survivors of the Holocaust.

00:39:47.420 --> 00:39:54.830
 And, um, one of the things that they talk about in this movie, he said, was that one of the reasons

00:39:54.830 --> 00:39:59.950
 and, you know, being many, but one of the major reasons, and after interviewing all of these

00:39:59.950 --> 00:40:04.750
 survivors, they said, well, you know, what, what, what was it that helped you?

00:40:04.750 --> 00:40:10.590
 And they said that it was, they were going to, they dreamed and in their mind, they remember

00:40:10.590 --> 00:40:16.710
 as a strategy for survival, the food that we're going to eat when they got out of the concentration

00:40:16.710 --> 00:40:17.050
 camp.

00:40:17.050 --> 00:40:25.710
 When he told me that it was such a, it really struck me, you know, it was such an interesting

00:40:25.710 --> 00:40:32.470
 way of how your mind, you sort of have to have an inner strength in your mind visually to be

00:40:32.470 --> 00:40:39.430
 able to survive such horrible, like experiences like that, not being able to eat food and control

00:40:39.430 --> 00:40:40.170
 that, you know?

00:40:40.190 --> 00:40:44.930
 And so it's about being able to control your mind and your body that that's related to.

00:40:45.270 --> 00:40:52.600
 Um, and the other thing is that I thought, you know, a lot of it may be in similar way.

00:40:52.600 --> 00:40:56.040
 It was about survival for these guys.

00:40:56.040 --> 00:40:57.840
 It is about survival in prison.

00:40:57.840 --> 00:41:02.640
 You know, a lot of times, you know, it's a lot of it is very violent inside, you know?

00:41:02.760 --> 00:41:05.390
 And so they always have to be on their watch.

00:41:05.510 --> 00:41:07.010
 They always have to be on their feet.

00:41:07.110 --> 00:41:11.090
 They always have to be able to see how do they best survive.

00:41:11.310 --> 00:41:16.320
 They trade things, you know, even if it's not related to food, they'll barter things with

00:41:16.320 --> 00:41:16.840
 each other.

00:41:16.940 --> 00:41:21.400
 You know, I know some other guys in conversation that would tell me, well, you know, if I don't

00:41:21.400 --> 00:41:26.550
 have any soap or any shaving cream, you know, when they're razor or whatever, I'll trade

00:41:26.550 --> 00:41:32.630
 and, you know, he spots me and, you know, and so I, I, I thought about that idea of food

00:41:32.630 --> 00:41:37.220
 and it's basic kind of idea of survival.

00:41:37.850 --> 00:41:44.720
 And, um, I, I grew up part of that time in Mexico as well.

00:41:44.780 --> 00:41:47.860
 I've been, I grew up my childhood, pretty much.

00:41:47.860 --> 00:41:56.100
 I grew up in Mexico, so I've had experiences in terms of, you know, being able to really

00:41:56.100 --> 00:42:06.640
 have that necessity of food and what that's like and, um, being able to say, we have this

00:42:06.640 --> 00:42:07.860
 much to eat today.

00:42:08.000 --> 00:42:09.950
 We don't have meat, but we have this much.

00:42:10.330 --> 00:42:12.610
 Um, how do we make it work?

00:42:12.850 --> 00:42:20.910
 And I, I had a great family, you know, my, my uncle would say to me, he would say, whatever

00:42:20.910 --> 00:42:23.470
 you have, you know, there's more than enough.

00:42:23.550 --> 00:42:25.070
 When there's for one, there's for two.

