 And sometimes even like the neighborhood kids would come and we would be able to say, okay,

00:42:30.370 --> 00:42:31.870
 we're going to sit down to eat.

00:42:31.930 --> 00:42:34.910
 It's like, I have this two tortillas we're going to share.

00:42:35.130 --> 00:42:37.740
 And I think it's also about that.

00:42:37.860 --> 00:42:44.090
 And then it wasn't still as a young person in those values and about how that was so evident

00:42:44.090 --> 00:42:49.330
 in my mind, because that was the approach that I always had.

00:42:49.470 --> 00:42:53.820
 You know, he would say, you know, if you don't, if you don't have that,

00:42:53.960 --> 00:42:55.600
 how are you going to make it work?

00:42:55.800 --> 00:42:58.060
 What is going to be your strategy for that survival?

00:42:58.400 --> 00:43:03.170
 You know, without food, I have to tell you, and I'm going to be really honest, like I,

00:43:03.330 --> 00:43:09.670
 we were poor, you know, but I know there were a lot of other kids and people that were really,

00:43:09.830 --> 00:43:10.410
 really starving.

00:43:10.750 --> 00:43:12.960
 Thank God we had our basic, basic stuff.

00:43:12.960 --> 00:43:14.700
 Cause I used to work as a kid in Mexico.

00:43:15.870 --> 00:43:18.300
 It's not like your child labor laws.

00:43:18.400 --> 00:43:22.690
 I mean, as a little kid, I used to work and, you know, help my family, whatever little

00:43:22.690 --> 00:43:25.370
 thing I could, you know, to, to try to make ends meet.

00:43:25.670 --> 00:43:31.050
 So I've seen a lot of that stuff, Chelsea, in terms of like being able to put value of

00:43:31.050 --> 00:43:37.130
 that survival and what it is to really the necessity of food and bear food.

00:43:37.630 --> 00:43:45.400
 And when I came to this country, you know, um, I think it was like 10 years old, it was such

00:43:45.400 --> 00:43:53.540
 an abundance of food, of things that I, I really kind of didn't know what to do.

00:43:54.760 --> 00:44:02.960
 You know, it was like, wow, like you have more than one, you know, and even, even just

00:44:02.960 --> 00:44:08.170
 like fast food, basic fast food, it was like such a big quantity of stuff, you know?

00:44:08.170 --> 00:44:13.220
 I mean, of course it's like, there's also a hierarchy in terms of like the social class

00:44:13.220 --> 00:44:18.470
 and like a lot of countries, but in particular, I'm just talking about Mexico in terms of like

00:44:18.470 --> 00:44:23.450
 the very poor and the very rich and the very rich is very exhaustive where they have a lot

00:44:23.450 --> 00:44:26.230
 of power and they do have access to more obviously.

00:44:26.590 --> 00:44:33.760
 But so I'm really in terms of working with this idea of that, you mentioned this food

